\section{Introduction}\label{sec:compiler-evolution-intro}

The history of compiler technology is a story of increasing sophistication, from the hand-crafted, machine-specific compilers of the 1950s to the modular, retargetable, verified, and self-optimizing systems of today. Each era brought new abstractions, new algorithms, and new architectures that transformed how compilers are built and how they perform.

This chapter traces the major revolutions in compiler technology: from the early days of Fortran I through the back-end optimization revolution, the retargetability movement, the SSA revolution, the verification movement, the JIT revolution, and the MLIR revolution. We draw lessons from each era and look toward future directions.

\section{Early Compilers: Fortran I}\label{sec:early-compilers}

\subsection{The Fortran I Compiler (1957)}

The Fortran I compiler, developed by John Backus and his team at IBM, was a landmark achievement. It was the first compiler to generate code that could compete with hand-written assembly in performance. The team's innovations included:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Symbolic addressing}: The compiler resolved symbolic addresses to machine addresses, freeing the programmer from manual memory management.
  \item \textbf{Expression optimization}: The compiler analyzed expressions to minimize redundant computations.
  \item \textbf{Register allocation}: A simple but effective algorithm assigned variables to registers.
  \item \textbf{Loop optimization}: The compiler recognized simple loop patterns and generated efficient code.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{historical}
The Fortran I compiler was 25,000 lines of assembly code, written for the IBM 704. It took 18 person-months to develop---an extraordinary investment at the time. The team included several women programmers---Jean Sammet, Marlyn Wescoff, and others---whose contributions were essential but often uncredited.
\end{historical}

\subsection{Limitations of Early Compilers}

Early compilers had significant limitations by modern standards:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{No intermediate representation}: The compiler translated directly from source to machine code.
  \item \textbf{Single-pass}: Most early compilers were single-pass, processing the source code once from beginning to end.
  \item \textbf{Machine-specific}: Each compiler targeted a specific machine; there was no concept of retargetability.
  \item \textbf{Limited optimization}: Optimization was ad hoc, based on pattern matching rather than systematic analysis.
  \item \textbf{No error recovery}: A single syntax error typically terminated compilation.
\end{itemize}

\section{The Back-End Revolution: Optimization}\label{sec:back-end-revolution}

\subsection{The Phase Architecture}

The most important architectural innovation in compiler design was the separation of the compiler into \emph{phases}:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Front end}: Lexical analysis, parsing, semantic analysis. Language-specific.
  \item \textbf{Middle end}: Machine-independent optimization. Language-independent.
  \item \textbf{Back end}: Instruction selection, register allocation, instruction scheduling. Machine-specific.
\end{enumerate}

This separation, which emerged in the 1970s and 1980s, enabled \emph{retargetability}: the same front end could be paired with different back ends for different machines, and the same back end could be used with different front ends for different languages.

\subsection{Dataflow Analysis}

The formalization of \emph{dataflow analysis} in the 1970s (by Kildall, Kam, Ullman, and others) provided a systematic framework for program optimization. Key contributions:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Reaching definitions}: Which definitions of a variable may reach a given point.
  \item \textbf{Available expressions}: Which expressions have already been computed.
  \item \textbf{Live variables}: Which variables are live at each point.
  \item \textbf{The iterative algorithm}: A general framework for solving dataflow equations on control-flow graphs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Global Optimization}

The 1980s saw the development of sophisticated global optimization techniques:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Loop-invariant code motion}: Moving computations out of loops.
  \item \textbf{Induction variable elimination}: Replacing complex induction variables with simple ones.
  \item \textbf{Global common subexpression elimination}: Removing redundant computations across basic blocks.
  \item \textbf{Global value numbering}: Identifying equivalent computations using value graphs.
  \item \textbf{Interprocedural analysis}: Analyzing across procedure boundaries.
\end{itemize}

Muchnick's \textit{Advanced Compiler Design and Implementation}~\cite{muchnick1997advanced} remains the definitive reference for this era of compiler optimization.

\subsection{The ``Dragon Book'' and Compiler Education}

Aho, Sethi, and Ullman's \textit{Compilers: Principles, Techniques, and Tools} (1986)---the ``Dragon Book''~\cite{aho2007compilers}---standardized compiler education. Its treatment of parsing, semantic analysis, intermediate representations, and code generation became the template for compiler courses worldwide.

\section{The Retargetability Movement}\label{sec:retargetability}

\subsection{The Problem of Machine Proliferation}

As the number of target architectures grew (x86, ARM, MIPS, SPARC, PowerPC, and many more), the cost of writing a separate compiler for each architecture became prohibitive. The retargetability movement sought to reduce this cost by separating machine-dependent and machine-independent components.

\subsection{Key Retargetable Compiler Projects}

\begin{description}
  \item[PCC (Portable C Compiler, 1978)] The first widely used retargetable compiler. Johnson's approach used a machine-description table to describe the target architecture.
  \item[GCC (GNU Compiler Collection, 1987)] Richard Stallman's compiler, originally for C, later extended to C++, Fortran, and many other languages. GCC's machine descriptions (``md files'') describe the target architecture's instructions, registers, and calling conventions.
  \item[lcc (1989)] Fraser and Hanson's compiler demonstrated that a retargetable compiler could be small and efficient. lcc's back end was described by a compact machine specification.
  \item[LLVM (2000)] The LLVM project introduced a novel approach to retargetability based on a well-designed intermediate representation (LLVM IR) and a modular pass infrastructure.
\end{description}

\subsection{Machine Description Languages}

A key enabler of retargetability was the development of \emph{machine description languages}:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Table-driven approaches}: The target machine is described by tables of instructions, registers, and patterns. The compiler uses these tables to generate code.
  \item \textbf{Tree-pattern matching}: Instruction selection is performed by matching tree patterns (representing IR expressions) against machine instructions. Tools like \textsc{IBURG} and \textsc{BURG} automate this process.
  \item \textbf{Table-driven instruction scheduling}: The scheduler uses machine descriptions to determine instruction latencies, resource constraints, and dependencies.
\end{itemize}

\section{The SSA Revolution}\label{sec:ssa-revolution}

\subsection{The Invention of SSA}

\emph{Static Single Assignment} (SSA) form, introduced by Cytron et al.\ in 1989~\cite{cytron1989ssa}, revolutionized compiler optimization. In SSA form, every variable is assigned exactly once, and every use of a variable refers to exactly one definition.

\begin{definition}[SSA Form]\label{def:ssa}
A program is in \emph{Static Single Assignment} form if every variable is defined exactly once. At control-flow merge points, $\phi$-functions are inserted to merge alternative definitions:
\[
x_3 = \phi(x_1, x_2)
\]
means that $x_3$ takes the value of $x_1$ if control came from the first predecessor, or $x_2$ if from the second.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Why SSA Matters}

SSA form simplifies many compiler optimizations:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Def-use chains are explicit}: Each use of a variable refers to exactly one definition, making dataflow analysis simpler.
  \item \textbf{Sparse conditional constant propagation}: Constants can be propagated efficiently using the SSA graph.
  \item \textbf{Global value numbering}: Equivalent computations are easily identified.
  \item \textbf{Dead code elimination}: A definition is dead if and only if it has no uses.
  \item \textbf{Loop-invariant code motion}: The dominance tree provides a natural framework for hoisting.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{SSA Construction and Destruction}

The construction of SSA form requires:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Computing the \emph{dominance frontier} of each basic block.
  \item Inserting $\phi$-functions at dominance frontiers.
  \item Renaming variables to ensure single assignment.
\end{enumerate}

The destruction of SSA form (converting back to conventional form) requires replacing $\phi$-functions with copy instructions, typically at the edges of the control-flow graph.

\subsection{Beyond Classical SSA}

Several extensions of SSA have been developed:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Gated SSA}: Adds predicates to $\phi$-functions, enabling more precise analysis.
  \item \textbf{Static single information} (SSI): Restricts SSA to track only variables with a single piece of information.
  \item \textbf{Array SSA}: Extends SSA to arrays, enabling analysis of array-based programs.
  \item \textbf{Heap SSA}: Extends SSA to heap-allocated objects.
\end{itemize}

\section{The Verification Movement}\label{sec:verification-movement}

\subsection{The Problem of Compiler Bugs}

Compilers are complex programs, and like all complex programs, they contain bugs. A compiler bug can produce incorrect code that is difficult to detect---the compiled program may appear to work correctly on most inputs but fail on others.

\subsection{Translation Validation}

\emph{Translation validation}~\cite{pnueli2001translation} checks that each individual compilation is correct, by comparing the source and target programs:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Run the compiler to produce target code.
  \item Verify that the target code is equivalent to the source code.
  \item If the verification succeeds, the compilation is correct.
  \item If it fails, report the error.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Certified Compilers}

A \emph{certified compiler} comes with a machine-checked proof that it always produces correct code. The CompCert project~\cite{leroy2009compcert}, developed by Xavier Leroy and colleagues, produced a certified C compiler using the Coq proof assistant.

\begin{theorem}[CompCert Correctness]\label{thm:compcert}
For any source program $s$ and compiled program $t = \mathit{CompCert}(s)$, if $s$ has defined behavior, then $t$ has the same observable behavior as $s$.
\end{theorem}

This theorem is proved in Coq, providing a machine-checked guarantee of correctness.

\subsection{Verified Optimization Passes}

The verification approach has been extended to individual optimization passes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Vellvm}: A verified LLVM front end in Coq.
  \item \textbf{CakeML}: A verified compiler for a subset of ML, proved correct in HOL4.
  \item \textbf{Pulse}: A framework for verifying optimization passes in a dependently typed language.
\end{itemize}

\section{The JIT Revolution}\label{sec:jit-revolution}

\subsection{From Static to Dynamic Compilation}

The JIT revolution began in the mid-1990s with the Java Virtual Machine. The key insight was that run-time information (type information, profiling data, class hierarchy) could be used to generate better code than a static compiler.

\subsection{Key JIT Innovations}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Inline caching}: Method call sites are specialized to the observed receiver type.
  \item \textbf{Polymorphic inline caching}: Call sites handle multiple observed types efficiently.
  \item \textbf{On-stack replacement (OSR)}: Running code can be replaced with optimized versions without restarting.
  \item \textbf{Deoptimization}: If speculative assumptions are violated, optimized code is replaced with a safe fallback.
  \item \textbf{Tiered compilation}: Code progresses through multiple tiers of optimization.
  \item \textbf{Tracing}: Hot loops are traced and compiled as a unit, enabling cross-inlining.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Modern JIT Compilers}

\begin{description}
  \item[HotSpot (Java)] Oracle's JVM uses tiered compilation with C1 (client) and C2 (server) compilers.
  \item[V8 (JavaScript)] Google's JavaScript engine uses Maglev (mid-tier) and TurboFan (optimizing) compilers.
  \item[SpiderMonkey (JavaScript)] Mozilla's engine uses IonMonkey for optimizing compilation.
  \item[GraalVM] A polyglot virtual machine with the Graal compiler, supporting multiple languages via the Truffle framework.
\end{description}

\section{The MLIR Revolution}\label{sec:mlir-revolution}

\subsection{The Problem of IR Proliferation}

By the 2010s, the compiler ecosystem faced a new problem: \emph{IR proliferation}. Every compiler framework had its own intermediate representation:
\begin{itemize}
  \item LLVM IR for systems languages
  \item HIR/MIR for Rust
  \item JSGIR for JavaScript
  \item Custom IRs for machine learning frameworks (TensorFlow, PyTorch)
  \item Custom IRs for hardware description (HLS, Chisel)
\end{itemize}

Each IR was designed for a specific purpose, and translating between them was expensive and lossy.

\subsection{MLIR: Multi-Level Intermediate Representation}

\emph{MLIR} (Multi-Level Intermediate Representation), developed at Google and open-sourced in 2019, addresses IR proliferation through a \emph{hierarchy of dialects}:

\begin{definition}[MLIR Dialect]\label{def:mlir-dialect}
A \emph{dialect} in MLIR is a set of operations, types, and attributes that form a coherent level of abstraction. Dialects can be:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{High-level}: Close to the source language (e.g., TensorFlow operations).
  \item \textbf{Mid-level}: Language-independent (e.g., loop nests, affine transformations).
  \item \textbf{Low-level}: Close to the hardware (e.g., LLVM operations, vector operations).
\end{itemize}
Lowering proceeds by translating high-level dialects to lower-level ones, preserving semantics at each step.
\end{definition}

