\subsection{Auditing Milestones by the Event They Actually Date}

Timeline rows compress a history of prototypes, papers, distributions,
and later adoption. Two dates can both be defensible for a technical
idea if they describe different events. To improve a timeline, make
each proposed row testable against a primary artifact rather than
treating any early mention as the release of a working tool.

\textbf{Contract.} Given a proposed row with a subject, event kind,
and year, inspect reviewed artifact records for that subject. Each
record has a dated event, an evidence locator, and a kind such as
publication, implementation report, or public release. Return the
same-kind support for the year, conflicting same-kind records, and
distinct event kinds separately. An empty result means evidence is
missing, not that the event did not occur.

\begin{sudocode}[Audit the date of one event type]
\Let{(Y,K,S)}{\text{row year, event kind, and subject}}
\Let{(A,C,O)}{\text{three empty evidence lists}}
\For{each record $r$ on subject $S$}
  \To{Use the date of the documented event, not the scan.}
  \If{$r.\mathrm{kind}\ne K$}
    \State append $r$ to $O$
  \ElsIf{$r.\mathrm{year}=Y$}
    \State append $r$ to $A$
  \Else
    \To{Preserve discrepancies instead of averaging dates.}
    \State append $r$ to $C$
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return $(A,C,O)$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} Suppose a row is labeled ``publication of
Knuth's LR parsing paper, 1962.'' The issue of \textit{Information
and Control} containing \textit{On the Translation of Languages
from Left to Right} is dated 1965. With that as the only reviewed
record, the output has zero matching records, one discrepancy
at \(1965\), and zero other events. This supports dating that
publication to 1965, without deciding whether earlier work deserves
another row.

The invariant is that each candidate record is classified once
and retains its locator. The audit is linear in the records
consulted. For multiple stages of one project, specify which
release or paper is claimed; a single subject heading is too
coarse to distinguish them. A priority claim such as ``first
retargetable compiler'' would require a separate search and a
definition of retargetability.

\section{The Human Side of Compiler Development}\label{sec:compiler-human}

\subsection{Compiler Communities}

Each major compiler project has developed a distinctive community culture:

\begin{description}
  \item[LLVM community] Emphasizes clean code, thorough testing, and inclusive design. The LLVM Developer Meeting is a major annual event.
  \item[GHC community] Academic in orientation, with strong ties to Haskell research. Development is driven by proposals and community consensus.
  \item[GCC community] Older and more established, with a strong tradition of maintaining backward compatibility.
  \item[Rust community] Emphasizes safety, correctness, and community governance. The Rust compiler team uses a mentorship model.
\end{description}

\subsection{The Art of Compiler Debugging}

Compiler bugs are among the most difficult to diagnose, because the compiler's output (compiled code) may appear to work correctly on most inputs but fail on others. Techniques for compiler debugging include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Delta debugging}: Automatically minimize the test case that triggers the bug.
  \item \textbf{Reduction}: Reduce the failing program to a minimal reproducer.
  \item \textbf{Bisection}: Use \texttt{git bisect} to find the commit that introduced the bug.
  \item \textbf{Optimization bisection}: Disable optimization passes one at a time to identify the buggy pass.
  \item \textbf{Translation validation}: Compare the output of the compiler against a reference implementation.
\end{itemize}
